      "Building a plan means turning lot size and inspection level into a sample size code letter, then reading the accept and reject numbers for the chosen AQL. In ISO 2859-1, a lot of 1201 to 3200 units at general inspection level II gives code letter K, which means n = 125. At AQL 1.0 % defective under normal single sampling, Ac = 3 and Re = 4: inspect 125 units, accept on 3 or fewer defective units, reject on 4 or more.",
      "Read the level and the lot band together, never one without the other. The same lot band gives code letter H (n = 50, Ac = 1, Re = 2 at AQL 1.0 %) at level I and code letter L (n = 200, Ac = 5, Re = 6 at AQL 1.0 %) at level III. The 3000-unit lot in the worked example sits inside 1201 to 3200, which is why it resolves to K; 5,000 units would fall into the next band, 3201 to 10000, and give L with n = 200."
